# Vercel and deploy economy

The project **auto-deploys on every push to `main`** (GitHub → Vercel).  
**Build minutes cost money.** Treat every deployment as opt-in.

## Non-negotiable

- **Do not auto-deploy.** Never run `git push`, `git push origin main`, or any remote update that would trigger Vercel **unless the user explicitly asks in this conversation** (e.g. "push", "deploy", "go live", "ship it", "push to main").
- **Always ask first** before a deploy or push. If work is done locally, **stop and ask** — do not push as a "default" or to "save the user a step."
- **Do not** run `vercel`, `vercel deploy`, `vercel --prod`, or any Vercel CLI command that creates a deployment without the same **explicit** approval.
- **Be economical:** prefer `npm run build` and `tsc` locally; avoid repeated pushes; batch changes into one push when the user is ready, instead of many small deploys.

## Always allowed (no Vercel cost)

- `npm run build`, `npm run lint`, `npm run typecheck` locally
- `git add`, `git commit`, `git status`, `git diff`, `git log`
- `npm run dev` and local testing

## When work is done

1. Summarize what changed.
2. Give the local commit hash if you committed.
3. **Ask** whether they want to push to `main` (and note that it will use Vercel build minutes).

## Approvals

- **Each** push to a branch that deploys needs **fresh** user approval, unless their message clearly authorizes a push/deploy right then.
- If they say "never auto deploy" or "ask first always," treat that as a standing requirement: **still confirm** before the first push after that when deploying would be the natural next step.
